1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,144
The second way into the configuration memory is the one bob load uses by default: AMD-style frames, carried in packets, exactly as a seven-series bitstream reaches a real part.

2
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:15,699
Why have a second path at all?

3
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:23,377
The project was asked for frame-based writing just like AMD's, slightly simplified, and frames buy three things.

4
00:00:23,697 --> 00:00:28,481
The stream finds its own start, so it can be split however the host likes.

5
00:00:28,801 --> 00:00:33,059
Frames are addressable, so part of the memory can be rewritten or read.

6
00:00:33,379 --> 00:00:38,026
And the stream checks itself, with a CRC and the device's identity.

7
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,690
The controller starts out hunting.

8
00:00:42,010 --> 00:00:53,344
It slides a thirty-two bit window along the incoming bits, one bit at a time, until the window equals the sync word, A A nine nine five five six six, AMD's own value.

9
00:00:53,664 --> 00:00:56,151
Dummy words before it are simply ignored.

10
00:00:56,633 --> 00:01:02,163
From then on every thirty-two bits is a word, however the host splits the stream into scans.

11
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:05,987
After the sync word come packets.

12
00:01:06,307 --> 00:01:11,520
Here is one header, three zero zero zero eight zero zero one, decoded.

13
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:13,951
The top three bits say type one.

14
00:01:14,271 --> 00:01:15,885
The next two say write.

15
00:01:16,205 --> 00:01:20,057
Five bits name a register: four, the command register.

16
00:01:20,377 --> 00:01:23,527
And the low eleven bits say one data word follows.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,735
So the next word is written to the command register: seven, which resets the CRC.

18
00:01:30,055 --> 00:01:38,186
For long writes like frame data, a type one header with a count of zero is followed by a type two header that carries a twenty-seven bit count.

19
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:42,077
The parser is a small state machine.

20
00:01:42,397 --> 00:01:44,721
Once synchronised it expects a header.

21
00:01:45,041 --> 00:01:50,942
A write with a count goes straight to the data state; a count of zero first expects a type two header.

22
00:01:51,262 --> 00:01:54,521
After the last data word it expects a header again.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:05,012
Anything wrong, an unknown header, register or command, a CRC or IDCODE mismatch, sends it to the error state, which only JPROGRAM can leave.

24
00:02:05,332 --> 00:02:07,872
The DESYNC command returns it to hunting.

25
00:02:08,192 --> 00:02:13,002
A read has no data words in the input: it queues words for the output instruction.

26
00:02:14,567 --> 00:02:17,246
These are the registers a packet can address.

27
00:02:17,566 --> 00:02:19,503
FAR holds the frame address.

28
00:02:19,823 --> 00:02:25,526
FDRI takes frame data, four words to a frame, and FDRO returns it.

29
00:02:25,846 --> 00:02:33,962
CRC checks integrity, IDCODE checks identity, STAT reports status, and CMD takes commands.

30
00:02:34,433 --> 00:02:50,061
The commands arm writes and reads, reset the CRC, mark the last frame, allow startup, return to hunting, and two that later milestones added: AGHIGH freezes the user clock, and GRESTORE loads every flip-flop's initial value.

31
00:02:50,381 --> 00:02:52,334
All the codes are AMD's own.

32
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:02,158
A frame address uses AMD's field layout: a block type, a row, a column, and a minor address, the frame within its column.

33
00:03:02,478 --> 00:03:09,806
Block type zero is configuration; block type one, since milestone fifteen, is block RAM contents.

34
00:03:10,267 --> 00:03:18,842
After every frame the address moves on by itself, across column boundaries, so a single data packet can write a whole run of frames.

35
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:27,276
Every data word written, together with the five-bit address of the register it went to, feeds a running CRC.

36
00:03:27,596 --> 00:03:31,198
Near the end of the stream the host writes the value it expects.

37
00:03:31,518 --> 00:03:35,213
If they differ the parser stops, and startup is refused.

38
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:40,977
Any frame data clears the match, so a CRC check must follow the last frame.

39
00:03:41,297 --> 00:03:44,760
A corrupted or truncated stream cannot start a design.

40
00:03:46,333 --> 00:03:49,572
Here is the real stream that loads the counter example.

41
00:03:49,892 --> 00:04:00,223
Two dummy words, the sync word, reset the CRC, write the IDCODE so the device can check it, arm frame writes, then a frame address and the frame data.

42
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:11,082
Because JPROGRAM has just zeroed the memory, only frames containing a one are sent: 30 frames, 214 words instead of more than two thousand.

43
00:04:11,402 --> 00:04:16,417
Then the expected CRC, last frame, start, and desync.

44
00:04:16,737 --> 00:04:21,309
The host reads every frame back to check, and runs the startup sequence.

45
00:04:22,867 --> 00:04:25,778
Frames make partial reconfiguration possible.

46
00:04:26,098 --> 00:04:37,833
The host compares the new design with the old one, freezes the user clock with AGHIGH, waits for the acknowledgement, writes only the frames that differ, and sends LFRM with a fresh CRC.

47
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:41,369
Changing one truth table rewrites one frame.

48
00:04:41,689 --> 00:04:49,471
Every register keeps its value across the change, and the testbench proves it with a counter that holds while frozen and then carries on.

49
00:04:49,791 --> 00:04:53,277
If the CRC is wrong, the fabric stays frozen.

50
00:04:54,833 --> 00:05:01,953
AMD carries block RAM contents in the bitstream as their own block type, and since milestone fifteen bob does too.

51
00:05:02,273 --> 00:05:09,469
Each frame of block type one holds four consecutive words of a memory, each in the low eighteen bits of a thirty-two bit word.

52
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:19,198
So a whole design, configuration and memory contents, travels as one stream under one CRC, and is read back and compared after loading.

53
00:05:20,767 --> 00:05:24,839
The newest command makes something unusual possible: time travel.

54
00:05:25,159 --> 00:05:28,396
Freeze the design and read every flip-flop through CAPTURE.

55
00:05:28,716 --> 00:05:38,820
Later, write those values into the flip-flops' initial-value bits with partial frames, and issue GRESTORE, AMD's command that pulses global set-reset.

56
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:48,104
Every flip-flop jumps to its saved value; put the original frames back, release the clock, and the design carries on from the moment it was saved.

57
00:05:48,424 --> 00:05:52,594
Milestone twenty-six's separate INIT bit is what makes this exact.

58
00:05:54,167 --> 00:06:06,140
And where bob departs from the real thing, the specification says so: no pad frames, no encryption or compression, no error correction, and frames of four words instead of a hundred and one.

59
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:10,988
The structure, the codes and the checks are AMD's; the scale is bob's.

60
00:06:12,567 --> 00:06:16,338
The frame controller is one Verilog file and one Python model.

61
00:06:16,658 --> 00:06:25,310
Every expected value in its testbench comes from the model, so the two cannot drift apart, and thirty-eight mutants prove each guard is tested.

